WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.280
How does breadth-first search work?

2
00:00:02.280 --> 00:00:04.300
Use a first-in, first-out queue

3
00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:05.680
to explore a graph one

4
00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:06.900
distance layer at a time.

5
00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:08.960
With unweighted edges, the first

6
00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:10.560
discovery gives a shortest edge-count

7
00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:11.200
distance.

8
00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:13.820
Our directed graph has arrows

9
00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:15.080
from A to B and

10
00:00:15.080 --> 00:00:16.820
C, from B and C

11
00:00:16.820 --> 00:00:18.680
to D, then from D

12
00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:21.873
to E. We scan neighbours

13
00:00:20.060 --> 00:00:22.940
alphabetically, initialise all other distances

14
00:00:22.940 --> 00:00:25.894
to infinity, and enqueue A

15
00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:26.360
at distance zero.

16
00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:28.760
Remove A from the front

17
00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:31.060
and inspect its neighbours. Discover

18
00:00:31.060 --> 00:00:33.060
B and C, give each

19
00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:34.940
distance one and parent A,

20
00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:36.500
and enqueue them in that

21
00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:36.860
order.

22
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:40.140
Remove B next, leaving C

23
00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:42.660
waiting. Discover D at distance

24
00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:44.580
two, set its parent to

25
00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:46.220
B, and add it behind

26
00:00:46.220 --> 00:00:48.380
C; the queue now contains

27
00:00:48.380 --> 00:00:50.180
C followed by D.

28
00:00:50.660 --> 00:00:53.040
Remove C and inspect its

29
00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:54.720
arrow to D. D was

30
00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:56.619
marked when it was enqueued,

31
00:00:56.500 --> 00:00:57.800
so skip it rather than

32
00:00:57.800 --> 00:01:00.591
adding a duplicate; the queue

33
00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:01.640
now contains only D.

34
00:01:02.380 --> 00:01:04.260
Remove D and discover E

35
00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:06.160
at distance three, with parent

36
00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:08.700
D. Then remove E, which

37
00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:10.960
has no undiscovered neighbours, and

38
00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:12.280
the queue becomes empty.

39
00:01:12.820 --> 00:01:14.220
To recover a route to

40
00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:16.860
E, follow parents backwards: E,

41
00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:18.720
D, B, A, then reverse

42
00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:20.720
that list. The route A,

43
00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:22.240
B, D, E uses three

44
00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:24.380
edges, matching the distance label.

45
00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:27.420
Why is first discovery optimal?

46
00:01:28.180 --> 00:01:30.000
The queue processes all vertices

47
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:31.920
at distance d before vertices

48
00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:33.580
at distance d plus one.

49
00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:35.500
Any shorter route to a

50
00:01:35.500 --> 00:01:37.220
newly discovered vertex would have

51
00:01:37.220 --> 00:01:38.300
offered it from an earlier

52
00:01:38.300 --> 00:01:39.020
layer already.

53
00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:42.220
Let n count vertices and

54
00:01:42.220 --> 00:01:45.260
m count edges. Initialisation costs

55
00:01:45.260 --> 00:01:47.740
order n, each vertex enters

56
00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:49.140
the queue at most once,

57
00:01:49.540 --> 00:01:51.460
and adjacency lists scan each

58
00:01:51.460 --> 00:01:54.020
directed edge once; undirected lists

59
00:01:54.020 --> 00:01:55.520
scan each edge twice.

60
00:01:55.900 --> 00:01:57.960
Distances, parents, and the queue

61
00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:00.400
together need order n auxiliary

62
00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:02.300
space, separate from the graph

63
00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:05.480
representation. An adjacency matrix instead

64
00:02:05.480 --> 00:02:07.480
scans n entries per expanded

65
00:02:07.480 --> 00:02:09.460
vertex, giving an n squared

66
00:02:09.460 --> 00:02:10.840
worst-case time bound.

67
00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:12.820
Things to take note of.

68
00:02:12.820 --> 00:02:14.780
Mark vertices at enqueue time,

69
00:02:14.780 --> 00:02:17.220
and distinguish discovery from expansion.

70
00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:19.600
Another neighbour order may change

71
00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:22.560
parents while preserving distances; unreachable

72
00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:24.100
vertices remain at infinity.

73
00:02:24.620 --> 00:02:26.840
Repeat from unvisited starts if

74
00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:28.660
you need all undirected components.

75
00:02:29.380 --> 00:02:31.500
Ordinary BFS does not solve

76
00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:34.000
arbitrary weighted shortest paths, because

77
00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:35.580
fewer edges need not mean

78
00:02:35.580 --> 00:02:37.400
less weight. Keep the queue

79
00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:38.740
honest, and the layers take

80
00:02:38.740 --> 00:02:39.520
care of themselves.
